#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:1
msgid "Return Address Authentication Hardening"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:3
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduction"

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:5
msgid ""
"Return Address Authentication Hardening is a mitigation against the PACMAN "
"attack, which aims to bypass Pointer Authentication on AArch64 targets. The "
"hardening mechanism described here is specific to pointer authentication of "
"return addresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:10
msgid ""
"Return Address Signing (or Authentication), also known as pac-ret, is a "
"feature devised to protect programs against Return Oriented Programming "
"(ROP), in which attackers may hijack the return address of functions in "
"order to direct execution to malicious code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:15
msgid "pac-ret can be enabled via different command-line options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:17
msgid ""
"`-mbranch-protection=` with `pac-ret`, `pac-ret+leaf` or `standard` as value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:19
msgid "`-msign-return-address=` with `non-leaf` or `all` as value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:20
msgid "`-fptrauth-returns`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:22
msgid ""
"More information can be found in [Pointer Authentication]"
"(PointerAuthentication.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:25
msgid ""
"Return Address Authentication Hardening is a mechanism to strengthen Return "
"Address Signing against the PACMAN attack in AArch64 targets. It can be "
"enabled with `-mharden-pac-ret=load-return-address`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:29
msgid "PACMAN attack"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:31
msgid ""
"PACMAN is an attack that aims to extract valuable information about pointer "
"authentication codes using side-channels in speculative execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:35
msgid ""
"It is performed with the use of gadgets to try and guess PAC codes. These "
"guesses raise no faults because they are done in speculation. By observing "
"the effects of the guessed PAC code on the processor's cache, it might be "
"possible to determine the valid PAC code for the address to which the "
"attacker wants the program to return."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:41
msgid "A usual PACMAN gadget looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:52
msgid "Such code would be compiled to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:63
msgid ""
"This code fragment may be used as a gadget. A speculative execution of this "
"fragment can happen as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:66
msgid ""
"If the Link Register (LR) has the right PAC code, `autiasp` will succeed and "
"strip the PAC code out of it. The processor's instruction fetcher will then "
"bring the code after the return into the cache (that is, the instructions "
"located at the address pointed by LR)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:71
msgid ""
"If the LR has the wrong PAC code, `autiasp` will not succeed and hence will "
"write a predefined error value to the LR's higher bits. Because of this, the "
"instruction fetcher will not bring the code after the return into the cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:76
msgid ""
"This difference in behavior is what drives the PACMAN attack. An attacker "
"can try to guess PAC codes and monitor cache behavior until the code after "
"the return is observed to have been brought into the cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:80
msgid "Details can be found at [pacmanattack.com](https://pacmanattack.com)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:82
msgid "Hardening"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:84
msgid ""
"In order to mitigate the PACMAN attack, a hardening mechanism can be enabled "
"with `-mharden-pac-ret=load-return-address`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:99
msgid ""
"The idea is to always bring the code after the return into cache (the "
"instructions located at the address pointed by LR), therefore minimizing the "
"difference between a speculative execution with a correct PAC code and with "
"an incorrect one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:104
msgid "`autiasp` performs the authentication step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:105
msgid ""
"`mov x8, x30` copies the return address (LR and x30 are synonyms) to a "
"temporary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:107
msgid "`xpaclri` strips the PAC code out of the return address in x30."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:108
msgid "`ldr w30, [x30]` performs a load from the return address in x30."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:109
msgid "`ret x8` returns to the authenticated return address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:111
msgid ""
"The load operation brings the code into the cache even if the authentication "
"step fails. As a consequence, in either case the code is loaded into the "
"cache. Furthermore, the return operation uses the original return address "
"before stripping, so the return address protection is still kept in place in "
"a normal non-speculative execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:117
msgid ""
"If FEAT_PAuth is present, the code sequence can make use of instructions "
"available with said feature with no change in semantics:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:128
msgid "Command-line option"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:130
msgid ""
"Return address authentication hardening can be enabled at translation unit "
"level with `-mharden-pac-ret=load-return-address`. It requires pac-ret to be "
"enabled at translation unit level as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:134
msgid "Function attribute"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:136
msgid ""
"In addition to the command-line option `-mharden-pac-ret=load-return-"
"address`, the developer can enable the mitigation at function level with the "
"use of the corresponding function attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:149
msgid "Caveats"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:151
msgid ""
"The load from the return address brings the code into the shared instruction/"
"data cache, therefore this cache level can't be used as an oracle to find "
"out whether the authentication succeeded or not. However, in the case of "
"authentication success, the code is also fetched into the instruction cache. "
"An attacker who is able to measure this cache level specifically may still "
"be able to carry out the exploit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ReturnAddressAuthenticationHardening.md:158
msgid ""
"Another caveat is if the code at the return address contains a load "
"operation within the speculation window. If this is the case, this load will "
"only execute speculatively if authentication succeeds, thus opening the "
"program up for exploitation despite the mitigation."
msgstr ""
